From: Mira <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: 2026-10-08
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2codecs: never copy a column-tiled frame

A film whose conformance window crops - 1920x808, say - sets need_crop,
which makes the H265 decoder copy every output frame. The copy maps the
buffer as though it were stored line by line and writes the result into a
plain linear one.

On the Raspberry Pi that is fatal twice over. The source is Broadcom's SAND
column layout, so reading it linearly scrambles it; and the linear form of
the 10-bit format, P030, is not importable by EGL at all, since v3d exposes
P030 only with the SAND128 modifier. The frame therefore never reaches the
screen: the film plays black with sound while the decoder runs flat out and
the sink reports hundreds of dropped frames.

Skipping the copy loses nothing. The driver is given the display size and
returns a buffer of exactly that height - 1212 rows per column for 808 lines
of luma plus 404 of chroma - so the hardware has already applied the crop.

The test is the invariant the importer downstream uses: a linear frame's
stride is at least its width, while a SAND frame reports the column height
there, which is smaller.

Signed-off-by: Mira <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/sys/v4l2codecs/gstv4l2codech265dec.c
+++ b/sys/v4l2codecs/gstv4l2codech265dec.c
@@ -990,6 +990,39 @@
     self->copy_frames = self->need_crop;
   }
 
+  /*
+   * Mira: a column-tiled frame must never be copied.
+   *
+   * The copy maps the buffer as if it were stored line by line, which a SAND
+   * frame is not, and writes the result into a plain linear buffer. On the
+   * Raspberry Pi that is fatal twice over: the pixels come out scrambled, and
+   * the linear form of the 10-bit format (P030) is not importable by EGL at
+   * all - v3d exposes P030 only with the SAND128 modifier - so the frame
+   * cannot reach the screen and the film plays black while the decoder runs
+   * flat out. Seen on a 1920x808 film, where the conformance window sets
+   * need_crop and so triggered the copy (2026-10-08).
+   *
+   * Cropping is not lost by skipping it here: the driver is given the display
+   * size and hands back a buffer of exactly that height - 1212 rows per
+   * column for 808 lines of luma plus 404 of chroma - so the crop has already
+   * been applied by the hardware.
+   *
+   * The test is the same invariant the importer uses: a linear frame's stride
+   * is at least its width, while a SAND frame reports the column height
+   * there, which is smaller.
+   */
+  if (self->copy_frames
+      && GST_VIDEO_INFO_PLANE_STRIDE (&self->vinfo, 0) > 0
+      && GST_VIDEO_INFO_PLANE_STRIDE (&self->vinfo, 0) <
+      (gint) GST_VIDEO_INFO_WIDTH (&self->vinfo)) {
+    GST_WARNING_OBJECT (self,
+        "Column-tiled output (stride %d < width %d): not copying frames, "
+        "the driver has already cropped to the display size.",
+        GST_VIDEO_INFO_PLANE_STRIDE (&self->vinfo, 0),
+        GST_VIDEO_INFO_WIDTH (&self->vinfo));
+    self->copy_frames = FALSE;
+  }
+
   return GST_FLOW_OK;
 }
 
